**NARRATIVE SUMMARY AGREEMENT**

This Narrative Summary Agreement (the “Agreement”) is made and entered into for the sole purpose of reciting, in binding summary form, the principal events, representations, and conditions set forth in the literary work commonly known as *The Catcher in the Rye*.

**RECITALS**

WHEREAS, Holden Caulfield, a sixteen-year-old individual (hereinafter “Holden” or the “Narrator”), has been expelled from Pencey Preparatory School for repeated academic failure and non-conformity with institutional expectations, and has elected to depart said premises several days prior to the scheduled holiday recess;

WHEREAS, Holden proceeds to New York City, where he engages in a series of transient encounters, including but not limited to: an unsuccessful attempt to arrange a date with a former acquaintance; an ill-advised transaction with a prostitute and her associate that results in physical and financial loss; conversations with former classmates and teachers that underscore his alienation; and repeated observations of what he characterizes as “phoniness” in adult society;

WHEREAS, Holden experiences recurring grief related to the prior death of his younger brother Allie, manifests protective concern for his younger sister Phoebe, and articulates a fantasy in which he stands in a field of rye catching children before they fall from a cliff (the “Catcher Fantasy”);

WHEREAS, following a visit with Phoebe and a period of physical and emotional deterioration, Holden narrates the foregoing events from a rest-home or similar institutional setting, without resolution of his underlying distress;

NOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the foregoing recitals, which are incorporated herein by reference, the essential narrative is summarized as the account of an adolescent’s expulsion, urban wandering, grief, disillusionment, and unresolved institutionalization, all as originally set forth by J. D. Salinger.

**IN WITNESS WHEREOF**, this Summary is tendered as a complete and accurate contractual-style condensation of the referenced work.
